  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Declassified Tea Tray",
    "overview": "\n**Creative North Star: \"The Declassified Tea Tray\"**\n\nA dark records room, lit by one phosphor-green lamp, where every tool sits on a tray in its own place. The room comes from the original CRT prototype: near-black blue surfaces, faint film grain, optional scanlines, a pixel wordmark and monospaced labels that read like stamped file headers. The tray is 茶盤 ちゃばん (`docs/design/chaban-design-style.md`): few controls, each shaped for its use, quiet motion, one accent that means \"on\".\n\nThe two layers have different jobs. The CRT layer is atmosphere and brand texture; it lives in the background, the wordmark and the type of metadata. The tea-tray layer governs everything a hand touches: controls, colour meaning, motion and words. When they disagree on a control, the tea tray wins. The evidence (scans, video frames, page text) is always the brightest, largest thing on screen; chrome steps back.\n\nDensity is high on purpose (archives are lists), but rows breathe: small mono metadata, generous card gaps, nothing decorative that doesn't help someone find or check a file.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Dark first; a warm paper light theme with AA-tuned text colours.",
      "One accent (`signal`, phosphor green by default; visitor can pick cyan, amber or violet) means on, active, primary.",
      "Mono for metadata and labels, grotesk for titles and reading, pixel only for the wordmark and page titles.",
      "Icon-only controls (lucide), words in `aria-label`/`title`.",
      "Flat surfaces separated by hairline borders, not shadows."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Lamp Rule",
        "body": "Only `signal` means \"on\". Never use it for decoration, headings or illustration.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Amber Means Trouble Rule",
        "body": "Amber in UI chrome only ever means \"failed, try again\".",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Themes Rule",
        "body": "Every colour pair must read in both themes; light text colours are AA-tuned (≥4.5:1 on `bg`), never reuse dark-theme hexes in light.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Stamp Rule",
        "body": "Anything that is metadata (ID, date, agency, count) is mono; anything that is meant to be read is grotesk.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Pixel Voice Rule",
        "body": "Press Start 2P is for the wordmark and page titles; never for body, buttons or long labels.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Tray Rule",
        "body": "Cards and controls never cast shadows. If something needs to look raised, use a thicker bottom edge; if pressed, sink it 2px and light it.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** read `docs/design/chaban-design-style.md` before touching any visitor-facing control.",
      "**Do** use theme tokens (`bg`…`signal`) via Tailwind names or `var(--…)`; never raw hexes in components.",
      "**Do** keep touch targets ≥36×36px (nav 44px) and icons 18px in 36px controls.",
      "**Do** write metadata in mono and reading text in grotesk (Stamp Rule).",
      "**Do** keep motion 120–200ms ease-out (sheets may use the 300ms `cubic-bezier(.32,.72,0,1)` slide) and stop it under `prefers-reduced-motion`.",
      "**Do** run colour pairs through `dataInk()` for data accents so light theme stays ≥4.5:1."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** use `signal` for decoration, headings or illustration (One Lamp Rule).",
      "**Don't** add shadows, glows or gradients to cards and controls; gradients are allowed only as a legibility scrim over media.",
      "**Don't** add new grain, scanline or pixel-font surfaces beyond the existing CRT layer; the room is set, the tray is where new work goes.",
      "**Don't** put visible text on controls; words go in `aria-label`, `title` and help.",
      "**Don't** let chrome outshine the evidence: scans and frames stay the largest, brightest element."
    ]
  }
